`getClusterTitleWidth` is declared as a plain (non-async) function, but `createLabel` is now async and returns a `Promise`. As written, `label` holds a `Promise` object, so `label.getBBox()` will throw `TypeError: label.getBBox is not a function` at runtime.

The author's own TODO comment on the preceding line confirms the concern. Fix:
```js
export const getClusterTitleWidth = async (elem, node) => {
  const label = await createLabel(elem, node.label, node.labelStyle, undefined, true);
  const width = label.getBBox().width;
  elem.node().removeChild(label);
  return width;
};
```
Note that the equivalent function in `dagre-wrapper/clusters.js` was correctly made `async`.

In the `startLabelRight` block, `createLabel` is called with `startEdgeLabelRight` as the parent element, but the element is immediately re-parented to `inner` on the very next line via `inner.node().appendChild(startLabelElement)`. Every other terminal label block (`startLabelLeft`, `endLabelLeft`, `endLabelRight`) passes `inner` directly to `createLabel`, making the subsequent `appendChild` unnecessary. The correct fix is:
```js
const startLabelElement = await createLabel(
  inner,  // <-- pass inner directly
  edge.startLabelRight,
  getLabelStyles(edge.labelStyle)
);
// remove the redundant: inner.node().appendChild(startLabelElement);
```
The same issue appears in `dagre-wrapper/edges.js`.

`getConfig()` is called twice inside the `setupImage` closure, which runs once per image element (i.e., inside a loop). Per project convention, `getConfig()` should be called at most once per function and the result reused.

Fix: hoist the call to the top of `configureLabelImages`:
```typescript
export async function configureLabelImages(container, labelText) {
  const config = getConfig();
  // ...
  const bodyFontSize = config.fontSize
    ? config.fontSize
    : window.getComputedStyle(document.body).fontSize;
}
```
